            <ArticleTitle>An acutely painful elbow as a first presentation of von Willebrand's disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>153-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 26 year old woman presented to the accident and emergency department with a painful right elbow. There had been no history of trauma. Clinical examination suggested an effusion, which was confirmed on radiological examination. Her elbow was aspirated and revealed a haemarthrosis. Subsequent investigations revealed a diagnosis of von Willebrand's disease (vWD). A spontaneously occurring effusion of the elbow may be due to a haemarthrosis. Aspiration of blood in the absence of trauma may lead to a diagnosis of an occult coagulopathy in addition to relieving pain. The diagnosis and treatment of vWD is discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
